00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  good morning welcome to day two of being  material the second biennial symposium  of MIT Center for art science and  technology in 1995 Nicholas Negroponte  II who joined us yesterday predicted a  future where being digital would mean  that our lives would be evermore shaped  by what he called the global movement of  weightless bits at the speed of light  negroponte use book tracked a shift of  actuation and signification occurring on  the level of atoms to a domain of  meeting created in bits yet unwieldy  matter persists and reasserts itself  during the symposium so far we've heard  about wearable technologies programmable  instruments and musical productions that  reflect the ways the properties of the  digital inflect our understanding of the  physical and material and vice-versa  in today's sessions on the livable and  invisible which I'm honored to co-chair  with Professor Stefan home rave we  continue to think in different ways with  artists scientists technologists  humanists and others about the tangible  thingy substances that comprise the  material world I'm pleased to introduce  the first panel this morning which will  take a variety of approaches toward the  world of living material from  researching living things to engineering  them understanding and managing  Assemblies of life are the core concerns  of a spectrum of academic study in  addition to corporate enterprise  politics and activism the environmental  conditions that foster life may be  treated as resources to be harnessed as  safeguards against scarcity the matter  of artistic inspiration or pattern forms  for thinking about the built environment  and the artistic and technological  design of urban landscapes was one of  the core concerns of capiche  when founding CA BS which we heard about  yesterday the category of the natural  environment may additionally be  understood as a resource that must be  actively preserved  a message foundational to earth day  which we celebrate this year with a  March for science at the conclusion of  today's symposium events and we will  have sign making activities and  materials available so that everybody  can participate in that  well the Boston March for science logo  features the Zakim bridge over the  Charles River  it's the massive bridge that we will  cross today as MIT anti-vietnam  protesters did in the 1960s surrounded  by sky art inflatables by former MIT  based artist Otto Pina  as I introduced today's panelists I'd  like to reflect on Tina's words about  the importance of materiality in an  increasingly immaterial sphere of  artistic practice  he said not all art has to be in  material to be mentally expansive and  mentally productive to deal with  immature reality for any artist you have  to know everything about materiality  this morning's panel on livable  materials assembles conversations about  the cultural incubation of life across  fields of biological design networked  ecology environmental remediation and  political economy we're joined today by  child anita director of the synthetic  biological systems laboratory at  columbia university tell us a synthetic  biologist to engineer some of the  smallest scale forms of life in the form  of dynamic environmentally responsive  programmable bacteria also with us today  is Claire Pentecost professor  photography at the school of the Art  Institute of Chicago Clare's artwork  involves research into agriculture  ecology and the tangle of relationships  between humans and other beings and  matter in her words we are walking  communities and our health is connected  to the world our third panelist this  morning is film our Dean of the School  of Social Sciences at the University of  California Irvine bill is a cultural  anthropologist of law property and  finance examining how monetary practices  commoditized unexpected aspects of  social biological and ecological life  his investigations of currency explored  the value placed on  trol resources in as much as the  parameters of the field of anthropology  the study of social and cultural life  itself all shedding new light on what it  means to make a living  conversations about the material  entanglements of body and environment  nature and culture resource harvesting  and environmental impact have become  ever more urgent here to frame matters  these matters of historical scientific  and cultural inquiry that comprised the  livable session is our moderator patinas  doser assistant professor in global  studies and languages at MIT  Bettina is an anthropologist who works  on the intersections of ecology  globalization and urban life she will  frame the panel to come with some  introductory reflections about how  livable material is defined from within  and without  through forces of desire appraisals of  value and assertions of solidarity  please join me in welcoming Bettina  up to the podium  [Applause]

